<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · region</p><h1 class="enc-title">Liaodong</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Liaodong is the region of north-east <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a> east of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/liao-river">Liao River</a>, including the Liaodong Peninsula between the Bohai Sea and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/korea">Korea</a> Bay. Held by the State of Yan and then by Chinese empires as a commandery, it was later contested by Goguryeo, the Khitan, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jurchens">Jurchen</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/manchus">Manchus</a>. In modern times its ports of Lüshun and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dalian">Dalian</a> were leased to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russia">Russia</a> and then to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/japan">Japan</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/liaodong.jpg" alt="Liaodong" width="320" height="320" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: LERK · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Location-of-Liaodong-Peninsula.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
